**What we learned**

- **Logistic regression beat gradient boosting on this dataset.** Best boosting pipeline (`{{best_boost_name}}`) minus logistic: PR-AUC {{boost_vs_lr_diff}} {{boost_vs_lr_ci}}. Fraud odds multiply with each extra red flag ({{flag_odds_multipliers}} going from 0→1, 1→2, 2→3 and 3→4+ flags), roughly the additive log-odds shape a logistic model captures directly; tuning pushed LightGBM towards very small trees and it still did not catch up. This is a statement about this data, not about fraud models in general.
- **SMOTE made things worse** (PR-AUC {{smote_diff}} {{smote_ci}} vs no resampling). SMOTENC sets categorical/boolean features by neighbour majority vote, so synthetic fraud carried red flags only {{smote_flag_ratio}}× as often as real fraud. Class weights ({{cw_diff}} {{cw_ci}}) and undersampling ({{us_diff}} {{us_ci}}) did not help either.
- **A decline tier did not clearly pay off.** Approve / step-up / decline looked cheaper on CV ({{tt_cv_diff}} per 1,000 transactions) but on the holdout the difference was {{ho_tt_diff}} {{ho_tt_diff_ci}}.
- **Fairness by age: {{fair_headline}}.** Step-up rate, recall, precision and false-positive rate per age band ({{fair_n_comparisons}} comparisons, Bonferroni) are all **{{fair_verdict}}**, which is not evidence of fairness. At the unadjusted 95% level {{fair_n_95}} gaps exclude 0 ({{fair_direction}}). Removing `customer_age` changes PR-AUC by {{noage_diff}} {{noage_ci}} ({{noage_verdict}}).
- **Splines and red-flag interactions did not help** ({{gam_diff}} {{gam_ci}} and {{pairs_diff}} {{pairs_ci}} vs logistic): the additive model is not visibly missing structure.
- **The threshold depends on the cost assumptions.** Scaling each cost ×0.5 or ×2 moves the best threshold between {{sens_thr_min}} and {{sens_thr_max}}.

